5 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186514126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514345 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514345 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514345 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186514126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514345 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f49d4659-1c96-5a65-a285-84ed00e2cd31" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514355 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514355 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514355 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514355 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514355 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514355 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514355 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514355 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514355 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514355 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514355 #edb/ref 1089 #edb/ref 17592186514356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514355 #edb/ref 1090 #uuid "d0365b9e-246c-5835-9ec1-5b3386ca8f07" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514356 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186514491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514356 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186514493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514356 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186514498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514356 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186514501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514356 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186514502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514356 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186514506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514356 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186514508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514356 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186514509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514356 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186514511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514356 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186514544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514356 #edb/ref 1085 "Consider the following functions: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x},\\,g(x)=8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3},\\,h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514356 #edb/ref 1086 #uuid "c918a931-a94f-57cf-9983-07568d2db58a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514356 #edb/ref 1087 #edb/ref 17592186514491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514384 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514384 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166078" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514384 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514384 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\int h(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,=${{field-1}}\n$+\\,C,\\,$ where $C$ is an arbitrary constant." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514384 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2932e26e-2b57-549a-866d-7abe171c7f20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514384 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int h(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514385 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514385 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166076" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514385 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514385 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $y=f(h(x)),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514385 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2d877a64-29c8-5e32-8920-d62abb73e697" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514385 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that $y$ is a composite function:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{h},\\,h=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}h}=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{h},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}h}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{h}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514409 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514409 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166072" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514409 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514409 #edb/ref 1071 "${g}^{″}(x)=${{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514409 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "60d8ee2b-aed9-5a87-bc1d-99d095c196bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514409 #edb/ref 1074 "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =8-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =8-2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the second derivative, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8-2{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514422 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514422 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166075" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514422 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514422 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h})\\,=${{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514422 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "709bef3c-1deb-5177-94a0-6a0cd6bca192" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514422 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}$ and $h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\nWe apply the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h}) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot h(x)-f(x)\\cdot {h}^{′}(x)}{[h(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})}^{′}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}}{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{16{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{16{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{8{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514423 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514423 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166079" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514423 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514423 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\int (4f(x)+3g(x))\\,\\text{d}x\\,=${{field-1}}\n$+C,\\,$ where $C$ is an arbitrary constant." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514423 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7323da16-6dcc-5329-81f1-5b6cb91bd332" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514423 #edb/ref 1074 "We combine the sum and the constant multiple rules, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4f(x)+3g(x))\\text{d}x & =\\int (4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}+3(8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}))\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2{e}^{x}+24x-2{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+24\\int x\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}+24\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+12{x}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514424 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514424 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166074" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514424 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514424 #edb/ref 1071 "The equation of the normal to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is$\\,y=$\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514424 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "745a29fd-db69-5896-9d5f-b9b3dcaa95b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514424 #edb/ref 1074 "When $x=\\pi ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & =f(\\pi )\\cdot h(\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{\\pi }\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{\\pi }\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn part 4, we found that the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is $m=2{e}^{\\pi }.$ Therefore, the slope of the normal ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{\\pi }}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & ={m}^{′}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{\\pi }}(x-\\pi ) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{2{e}^{\\pi }}+\\frac{\\pi }{2{e}^{\\pi }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514433 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514433 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166071" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514433 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514433 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}\\,=${{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514433 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "83dc5138-e470-5b8f-b97c-b3006ebeb428" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514433 #edb/ref 1074 "We know that \n\n$$\n\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nApplying this formula and the constant multiple rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514464 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514464 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166073" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514464 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514464 #edb/ref 1071 "If a curve $C$ is defined by the equation $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$\n\nSlope $\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514464 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d1eaed8d-b238-536c-b3a6-a6e7f9e94a12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514464 #edb/ref 1074 "We differentiate using the product rule. Recall that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}$ and $h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}+h\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\pi $ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\pi } & =-2{e}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{\\pi }(-1+0) \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $2{e}^{\\pi }.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514466 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514466 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514466 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166077" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514466 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514466 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/5d/5d2bfbefca5ce0a0b04accad183d0f43537d600c72000058b59f692398259a80.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=g(x)$ for $x\\in [-3,3]$ is plotted above. In ascending order, what are the $x$-coordinates of the points on this interval where ${g}^{′}(x)=0?$\n\n${g}^{′}(x)=0\\,$ when $\\,x\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x\\,=$\n{{field-2}}\n." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514466 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d78b3b66-caba-5b75-9179-7631fdc0e796" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514466 #edb/ref 1074 "A stationary point of $g(x)$ is a value of $x$ where ${g}^{′}(x)=0.$ Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal.  \n\nIn the given graph, we can see that at $x=-2$ and $x=2,$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${g}^{′}(x)=0$ at these points.\n\n![](images/36/36fd58470a5361250fa7852d92abe2b9c2dbf18ca97d00e51ea4013f8f3aa348.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, there are two stationary points, and their $x$-coordinates (in ascending order) are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514483 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514483 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166070" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514483 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514483 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $p(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\np(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}k+10, & \\,x\\le 3, \\\\ g(x), & \\,x>3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFind the value of the constant $k$ that makes $p(x)$ continuous at $x=3.$\n\n$k\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514483 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fbda5c07-3c3f-5393-8bd3-32f9b514a1ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514483 #edb/ref 1074 "We have the piecewise function\n\n$$\np(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}k+10, & \\,x\\le 3, \\\\ 8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}, & \\,x>3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAt $x=3,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}p(x) & =p(3) \\\\  & =k+10,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}p(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}(8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =8(3)-\\frac{2}{3}(3)^{3} \\\\  & =24-18 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $p(x)$ to be continuous at $x=3,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}p(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}p(x) \\\\ k+10 & =6 \\\\ k & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p(x)$ is continuous at $x=3$ when $k=-4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514491 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186514509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514491 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1cdb8aa6-8ecf-556b-897d-ed5387e45285" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514491 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186514483 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514493 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186514506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514493 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "20a1a95d-5d23-50cc-a5eb-c111e96d8376" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514493 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186514464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514498 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186514544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514498 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "49ad54e7-0681-59d7-bdbd-37e117d97c3e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514498 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186514466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514501 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186514511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514501 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "50fe811b-de32-57c9-9c1e-57847199bb20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514501 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186514422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514502 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186514493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514502 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "52c4219e-a8d2-540b-930a-53ae79e3d7aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514502 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186514409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514506 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186514501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514506 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6318fb84-c369-5d02-a31f-f2cf6cab22b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514506 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186514424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514508 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "69e9